267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 213 0 R
/T (Why relative addressing:  Instruction length is reduced as all the address information need not be included in the instructions By changing the content of R, processor can change the absolute addresses referred to by a block \(B\) of instructions Permits the processor to move \(relocate\) the entire block B from one region of memory to another without invalidating the addresses in B When used in this way, R may be referred to as a base register and its content as a base address)
/E (Why relative addressing:  Instruction length is reduced as all the address information need not be included in the instructions By changing the content of R, processor can change the absolute addresses referred to by a block \(B\) of instructions Permits the processor to move \(relocate\) the entire block B from one region of memory to another without invalidating the addresses in B When used in this way, R may be referred to as a base register and its content as a base address)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [26 27]
>>
endobj
